#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Judge time-scaling exponents over the board benches' criterion medians.

The amplification board (before::meter::board) judges deterministic
counters and liveness floors only, so its output is byte-identical under
any machine load. The implementation-agnostic witness for *time* — work in
plain machine words that no counter column can see — is judged here
instead, where timing belongs to a harness built for it: criterion owns
warmup, sampling, and outlier rejection, and this tool is plain
deterministic arithmetic over criterion's saved estimates.

The pipeline (`just bench-judge`): run `benches/board.rs` at two scales
with `--save-baseline` per scale, each run writing a denominator sidecar
(a configuration stamp plus cell ID -> the board's denominator bytes at
that scale, the board's own denomination read back from actual results);
then, per cell, fit

    e = ln(median_hi / median_lo) / ln(denom_hi / denom_lo)

and hold every judged cell to MAX_WALL_SCALING_EXPONENT. The fit is the
board's own scaling-exponent form (board.rs `exponent()`: log of the
measurement ratio over log of the denominator-bytes ratio) at the ceiling
that moved here with the leg — the same convention, not a new one. A cell
is judged only when its larger-scale median reaches
MIN_JUDGED_MEDIAN_NANOS (derivation at the constant); sub-floor cells are
enumerated as SKIP, never judged. Output is a red/green table in the
board's style (red rows first); exit is nonzero on any red (or, in roster
mode, on any roster violation).

Input-error discipline: exit 2, with the offending cell or file named,
never a scored verdict and never a silent skip. Input errors are a
malformed or mismatched pipeline, and scoring one would launder it into a
judgment: a nonpositive median at either scale, a non-finite (NaN or
infinite) median, a missing median in a saved estimate, a malformed
sidecar or roster, mismatched sidecar stamps, and a roster whose pinned
configuration is not the invocation's. (The board's zero-count clamps
have no analogue here because a criterion median is a positive float, so
none of these is ever a score.)

Sidecar stamps: each sidecar carries the configuration of the bench run
that wrote it — the resolved scale, the profile, the sampling mode, and
the source tip (`BOARD_BENCH_TIP`, the recipes pass `git rev-parse
HEAD`). The judge refuses (exit 2) two sidecars whose profiles, sampling
modes, or tips disagree, a scale pair that does not grow, and any stamp
that disagrees with `--tip`: a leftover baseline from an interrupted or
older run, or a sidecar rewritten at a different scale than the saved
medians, is a mismatch, never a silent re-score.

Profiles: criterion benches build under cargo's bench profile
(optimized), and the judged medians, this tool's records, and the roster
are all optimized-profile numbers; the amplification board's counter
records are dev-profile numbers — each artifact pins its own profile.
Verdicts are profile-sensitive, so the harness stamps the profile it
observed (`dev` under debug assertions, `optimized` otherwise) and the
judge refuses a roster pinned to a different one.

Roster mode (`--roster FILE`): the committed expected-verdict roster —
the board-side sibling of the gate's expected-failure test roster, same
membership-by-name philosophy — makes the judge enforce a *fixed* verdict
map instead of all-green, so `just all` stays meaningful while owned reds
await their cures. The file pins the configuration it was recorded under
(profile, sampling, scale pair; mismatch is exit 2) and lists cells in
two classes:

  - "red": must be judged and must read RED. Reading GREEN is a
    verdict-flip or liveness signal (exit 1, cell named): either the cure
    landed (re-baseline deliberately) or the pipeline stopped seeing the
    work. Reading SKIP is likewise a violation — drifting out of judgment
    is a change, not a pass.
  - "boundary": must be judged; either verdict is accepted while the
    fitted exponent stays within FIT_NOISE_EXPONENT_BAND of the ceiling
    (derivation at the constant). Outside the band the cell has genuinely
    moved and the roster entry is stale (exit 1). Reading SKIP is a
    violation, as above.

Any unrostered cell reading RED is a violation (exit 1). Unrostered cells
may drift between GREEN and SKIP freely — both are non-red, and
near-floor cells cross the judgment floor with machine noise. A roster
naming a cell the run does not contain is an input error (exit 2).

Usage:
    benchjudge --criterion-dir DIR --lo NAME --hi NAME \\
        --denoms-lo FILE --denoms-hi FILE \\
        [--roster FILE | --expect-red] [--tip HASH]
    benchjudge --self-test

`--expect-red` inverts the bottom line for tripwire runs (`just
bench-judge-tripwire`): exit 0 iff at least one judged cell is red.

`--self-test` (run at the head of every bench-judge recipe) pins the
judging arithmetic AND the exit contract end-to-end: it invokes `main` on
synthetic criterion trees and asserts the exit codes themselves — 0 on
all-green, 1 on a red, 1 on a dark tripwire, 1 on each roster violation,
2 on each input error — so a judge that stops failing cannot pass its own
self-test.
"""

import argparse
import contextlib
import io
import json
import math
import sys
from pathlib import Path

# The ceiling on every judged cell's fitted time exponent. Generous so
# scheduler noise on a linear cell cannot trip it, impassable for a
# quadratic's ~2.0.
MAX_WALL_SCALING_EXPONENT = 1.3

# Criterion's own resolution: its smallest timed unit is one batch of at
# least one iteration, read with a pair of monotonic-clock samples — two
# timer reads (~25 ns each) against a tick no coarser than ~42 ns on the
# deployed hosts (the 24 MHz Apple Silicon timebase) bound the per-batch
# measurement error at ~100 ns. Not a number invented here: it is the
# harness's floor, restated.
CRITERION_TIMER_ERROR_NANOS = 100

# The judged (larger-scale) median must dominate the timer error by this
# factor. The floor binds the hi median only — the lo median is
# deliberately unfloored, because a hi-side floor already excludes cells
# too small to time and a lo-side floor would evict real judged coverage
# (benign rows, the dense projection, and the cliff fork all judge from
# lo medians of 2-10 us). The honest resolution bound under that choice
# is FIT_NOISE_EXPONENT_BAND, below.
RESOLUTION_DOMINANCE_FACTOR = 100

# The judgment floor: cells whose larger-scale median sits below this are
# enumerated but never judged. Derived, not calibrated:
# CRITERION_TIMER_ERROR_NANOS x RESOLUTION_DOMINANCE_FACTOR = 10 us.
MIN_JUDGED_MEDIAN_NANOS = CRITERION_TIMER_ERROR_NANOS * RESOLUTION_DOMINANCE_FACTOR

# The scale pair's nominal denominator span: the record scale is x4, and
# the judged families' denominator bytes grow at least in proportion
# (most spans measure ~4.0; wider spans — the cliff comb's x15 — only
# shrink resolution's pull on the fit).
NOMINAL_DENOMINATOR_SPAN = 4.0

# How far timer resolution alone can pull a fitted exponent that sits at
# the ceiling — the fit-noise band the roster's boundary class accepts.
# Derived, not calibrated: a judged cell has median_hi >= the floor, so
# the hi-side relative error is at most 1/RESOLUTION_DOMINANCE_FACTOR
# (1%); a cell fitted AT the ceiling across the nominal x4 span has
# median_lo >= floor / 4^1.3, so its lo-side relative error is at most
# 4^1.3 / RESOLUTION_DOMINANCE_FACTOR (~6.1%). Worst one-direction pull:
#   ( ln(1 + 0.01) + ln(1 / (1 - 0.061)) ) / ln 4  ~ 0.052.
# Only near-ceiling cells matter for a verdict, so this is the operative
# bound: a cell whose true exponent sits farther than the band from the
# ceiling cannot be flipped by resolution.
FIT_NOISE_EXPONENT_BAND = (
    math.log1p(1.0 / RESOLUTION_DOMINANCE_FACTOR)
    - math.log1p(
        -(NOMINAL_DENOMINATOR_SPAN**MAX_WALL_SCALING_EXPONENT)
        / RESOLUTION_DOMINANCE_FACTOR
    )
) / math.log(NOMINAL_DENOMINATOR_SPAN)

# The stamp fields a sidecar must carry, and the vocabularies of the two
# enumerated ones (the profile vocabulary is the harness's observable:
# debug assertions on = dev, off = optimized).
STAMP_FIELDS = ("scale", "profile", "sampling", "tip")
PROFILES = ("dev", "optimized")
SAMPLINGS = ("quick", "record")


class JudgeError(Exception):
    """A malformed input: bad estimates, sidecars, stamps, or roster."""


def checked_median(name, side, value):
    """Validate one cell's median: positive and finite, or an input error."""
    if isinstance(value, bool) or not isinstance(value, (int, float)):
        raise JudgeError(f"{name}: {side} median is not a number ({value!r})")
    if not math.isfinite(value):
        raise JudgeError(f"{name}: {side} median is not finite ({value})")
    if value <= 0:
        raise JudgeError(f"{name}: {side} median is not positive ({value})")
    return value


def fit_exponent(median_lo, median_hi, denom_lo, denom_hi, name):
    """The board's scaling-exponent fit over one cell's two medians."""
    if denom_lo <= 0 or denom_hi <= denom_lo:
        raise JudgeError(
            f"{name}: denominator bytes must grow across the scales, "
            f"got {denom_lo} -> {denom_hi}"
        )
    return math.log(median_hi / median_lo) / math.log(denom_hi / denom_lo)


def judge_cells(cells):
    """Judge `(name, median_lo, median_hi, denom_lo, denom_hi)` rows.

    Returns `(verdict, name, exponent, row)` tuples where verdict is one of
    GREEN, RED, SKIP; the exponent is None for SKIP (a sub-floor median has
    no judged exponent). Malformed medians — nonpositive or non-finite at
    either scale — raise JudgeError before any floor comparison: an input
    error is never laundered into a sub-floor SKIP.
    """
    rows = []
    for name, median_lo, median_hi, denom_lo, denom_hi in cells:
        median_lo = checked_median(name, "lo", median_lo)
        median_hi = checked_median(name, "hi", median_hi)
        if median_hi < MIN_JUDGED_MEDIAN_NANOS:
            rows.append(
                (
                    "SKIP",
                    name,
                    None,
                    f"SKIP  {label(name)}  hi median {nanos(median_hi)} below the "
                    f"{nanos(MIN_JUDGED_MEDIAN_NANOS)} judgment floor",
                )
            )
            continue
        exponent = fit_exponent(median_lo, median_hi, denom_lo, denom_hi, name)
        verdict = "GREEN" if exponent <= MAX_WALL_SCALING_EXPONENT else "RED"
        rows.append(
            (
                verdict,
                name,
                exponent,
                f"{verdict:<5} {label(name)}  e {exponent:5.2f}  "
                f"n {denom_lo}->{denom_hi} B  "
                f"median {nanos(median_lo)}->{nanos(median_hi)}",
            )
        )
    return rows


def label(name):
    """Pad a cell ID into the board's op/family column widths."""
    op, _, family = name.partition("/")
    return f"{op:<24} {family:<12}"


def nanos(value):
    """Format a nanosecond reading compactly (ns/us/ms/s)."""
    for unit, scale in (("s", 1e9), ("ms", 1e6), ("us", 1e3)):
        if value >= scale:
            return f"{value / scale:.2f}{unit}"
    return f"{value:.0f}ns"


def read_sidecar(path):
    """Read a denominator sidecar: its stamp and its cell -> bytes map."""
    try:
        sidecar = json.loads(Path(path).read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
    except OSError as error:
        raise JudgeError(f"{path}: cannot read the sidecar: {error}") from error
    except json.JSONDecodeError as error:
        raise JudgeError(f"{path}: the sidecar is not JSON: {error}") from error
    if not isinstance(sidecar, dict) or set(sidecar) != {"stamp", "denominator_bytes"}:
        raise JudgeError(
            f"{path}: expected a JSON object with exactly a `stamp` and a "
            f"`denominator_bytes` member"
        )
    stamp = sidecar["stamp"]
    if not isinstance(stamp, dict) or set(stamp) != set(STAMP_FIELDS):
        raise JudgeError(f"{path}: the stamp must carry exactly {STAMP_FIELDS}")
    scale = stamp["scale"]
    if (
        isinstance(scale, bool)
        or not isinstance(scale, (int, float))
        or not math.isfinite(scale)
        or scale <= 0
    ):
        raise JudgeError(f"{path}: stamp scale must be a positive number, got {scale!r}")
    if stamp["profile"] not in PROFILES:
        raise JudgeError(f"{path}: stamp profile must be one of {PROFILES}")
    if stamp["sampling"] not in SAMPLINGS:
        raise JudgeError(f"{path}: stamp sampling must be one of {SAMPLINGS}")
    if stamp["tip"] is not None and not isinstance(stamp["tip"], str):
        raise JudgeError(f"{path}: stamp tip must be a string or null")
    denoms = sidecar["denominator_bytes"]
    if not isinstance(denoms, dict) or not denoms:
        raise JudgeError(f"{path}: expected a nonempty denominator_bytes object")
    for name, denom in denoms.items():
        if isinstance(denom, bool) or not isinstance(denom, int) or denom <= 0:
            raise JudgeError(
                f"{path}: {name}: denominator bytes must be a positive "
                f"integer, got {denom!r}"
            )
    return stamp, denoms


def cross_check_stamps(stamp_lo, stamp_hi, tip):
    """Refuse sidecars from different runs, trees, or configurations."""
    for field in ("profile", "sampling", "tip"):
        if stamp_lo[field] != stamp_hi[field]:
            raise JudgeError(
                f"the two sidecars disagree on {field}: "
                f"{stamp_lo[field]!r} vs {stamp_hi[field]!r} "
                f"(a leftover baseline from another run?)"
            )
    if stamp_lo["scale"] >= stamp_hi["scale"]:
        raise JudgeError(
            f"the scale pair must grow: lo stamped {stamp_lo['scale']}, "
            f"hi stamped {stamp_hi['scale']}"
        )
    if tip is not None:
        for side, stamp in (("lo", stamp_lo), ("hi", stamp_hi)):
            if stamp["tip"] != tip:
                raise JudgeError(
                    f"the {side} sidecar is stamped tip {stamp['tip']!r}, not the "
                    f"invocation's {tip!r}: a leftover baseline from another tree"
                )


def read_median(criterion_dir, name, baseline):
    """Read one cell's median point estimate (ns) from a saved baseline."""
    op, _, family = name.partition("/")
    path = Path(criterion_dir, op, family, baseline, "estimates.json")
    if not path.is_file():
        raise JudgeError(f"{name}: no saved estimates at {path}")
    try:
        estimates = json.loads(path.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
    except (OSError, json.JSONDecodeError) as error:
        raise JudgeError(f"{name}: cannot read estimates at {path}: {error}") from error
    try:
        return estimates["median"]["point_estimate"]
    except (TypeError, KeyError) as error:
        raise JudgeError(
            f"{name}: estimates at {path} carry no median point estimate"
        ) from error


def load_cells(criterion_dir, lo, hi, denoms_lo_path, denoms_hi_path, tip):
    """Assemble the judged rows from the two baselines and two sidecars."""
    stamp_lo, denoms_lo = read_sidecar(denoms_lo_path)
    stamp_hi, denoms_hi = read_sidecar(denoms_hi_path)
    cross_check_stamps(stamp_lo, stamp_hi, tip)
    if denoms_lo.keys() != denoms_hi.keys():
        difference = sorted(denoms_lo.keys() ^ denoms_hi.keys())
        raise JudgeError(f"the two sidecars disagree on the cell set: {difference}")
    cells = [
        (
            name,
            read_median(criterion_dir, name, lo),
            read_median(criterion_dir, name, hi),
            denoms_lo[name],
            denoms_hi[name],
        )
        for name in denoms_lo
    ]
    return (stamp_lo, stamp_hi), cells


def load_roster(path):
    """Read the expected-verdict roster: pinned configuration and classes."""
    try:
        roster = json.loads(Path(path).read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
    except OSError as error:
        raise JudgeError(f"{path}: cannot read the roster: {error}") from error
    except json.JSONDecodeError as error:
        raise JudgeError(f"{path}: the roster is not JSON: {error}") from error
    if not isinstance(roster, dict) or not {"configuration", "red", "boundary"} <= set(
        roster
    ) <= {"configuration", "red", "boundary", "notes"}:
        raise JudgeError(
            f"{path}: expected a JSON object with `configuration`, `red`, "
            f"`boundary`, and optionally `notes`"
        )
    configuration = roster["configuration"]
    wanted = {"profile", "sampling", "scale_lo", "scale_hi"}
    if not isinstance(configuration, dict) or set(configuration) != wanted:
        raise JudgeError(f"{path}: the configuration must pin exactly {sorted(wanted)}")
    for cls in ("red", "boundary"):
        names = roster[cls]
        if not isinstance(names, list) or not all(
            isinstance(name, str) for name in names
        ):
            raise JudgeError(f"{path}: `{cls}` must be a list of cell IDs")
    overlap = sorted(set(roster["red"]) & set(roster["boundary"]))
    if overlap:
        raise JudgeError(f"{path}: cells rostered both red and boundary: {overlap}")
    return roster


def check_roster_configuration(roster_path, configuration, stamp_lo, stamp_hi):
    """Refuse a roster whose pinned configuration is not this invocation's."""
    pins = (
        ("profile", configuration["profile"], stamp_lo["profile"]),
        ("sampling", configuration["sampling"], stamp_lo["sampling"]),
        ("scale_lo", configuration["scale_lo"], stamp_lo["scale"]),
        ("scale_hi", configuration["scale_hi"], stamp_hi["scale"]),
    )
    for field, pinned, actual in pins:
        if pinned != actual:
            raise JudgeError(
                f"{roster_path}: the roster pins {field} = {pinned!r} but this "
                f"run is stamped {actual!r}: the roster's expectations were "
                f"recorded under a different configuration"
            )


def roster_violations(roster, rows):
    """The named roster violations in `rows`, in row order.

    Every rostered cell must be judged with its class's verdict; any
    unrostered RED is a violation. A rostered cell absent from the run
    entirely is an input error, not a violation: the run and the roster
    describe different boards.
    """
    red = set(roster["red"])
    boundary = set(roster["boundary"])
    names = {name for _, name, _, _ in rows}
    missing = sorted((red | boundary) - names)
    if missing:
        raise JudgeError(f"the roster names cells this run does not contain: {missing}")
    violations = []
    for verdict, name, exponent, _ in rows:
        if name in red:
            if verdict == "GREEN":
                violations.append(
                    f"{name}: rostered red read GREEN (e {exponent:.2f}): a cured "
                    f"cell or a verdict-flip/liveness signal; re-baseline the "
                    f"roster deliberately"
                )
            elif verdict == "SKIP":
                violations.append(
                    f"{name}: rostered red fell below the judgment floor: "
                    f"drifting out of judgment is a change, not a pass"
                )
        elif name in boundary:
            if verdict == "SKIP":
                violations.append(
                    f"{name}: rostered boundary fell below the judgment floor: "
                    f"drifting out of judgment is a change, not a pass"
                )
            elif abs(exponent - MAX_WALL_SCALING_EXPONENT) > FIT_NOISE_EXPONENT_BAND:
                violations.append(
                    f"{name}: rostered boundary fitted e {exponent:.2f}, outside "
                    f"the +-{FIT_NOISE_EXPONENT_BAND:.3f} fit-noise band around "
                    f"the {MAX_WALL_SCALING_EXPONENT} ceiling: the cell has "
                    f"genuinely moved; re-roster it deliberately"
                )
        elif verdict == "RED":
            violations.append(f"{name}: unrostered cell read RED (e {exponent:.2f})")
    return violations


def render(rows, out):
    """Print the verdict table, red rows first, and the bottom line."""
    print(
        "bench-judge: time-scaling exponents over criterion medians "
        "(the amplification board's time leg)",
        file=out,
    )
    print(
        f"green iff fitted exponent <= {MAX_WALL_SCALING_EXPONENT} against the "
        f"board's denominator bytes; judged once the larger-scale median reaches "
        f"{nanos(MIN_JUDGED_MEDIAN_NANOS)} ({RESOLUTION_DOMINANCE_FACTOR} x the "
        f"~{CRITERION_TIMER_ERROR_NANOS} ns per-batch timer error)",
        file=out,
    )
    print(file=out)
    # Red rows first (the board's convention), then green, then sub-floor;
    # cell order within a verdict stays the sidecar's (board row order).
    order = ("RED", "GREEN", "SKIP")
    for wanted in order:
        for verdict, _, _, line in rows:
            if verdict == wanted:
                print(line, file=out)
    counts = {verdict: 0 for verdict in order}
    for verdict, _, _, _ in rows:
        counts[verdict] += 1
    print(file=out)
    print(
        f"bench-judge: {counts['GREEN']} green / {counts['RED']} red / "
        f"{counts['SKIP']} sub-floor ({len(rows)} cells)",
        file=out,
    )
    return counts


def self_test():
    """Pin the judging arithmetic and the exit contract end-to-end."""
    import tempfile

    floor = MIN_JUDGED_MEDIAN_NANOS

    def verdicts(cells):
        return [(verdict, name) for verdict, name, _, _ in judge_cells(cells)]

    # A linear cell reads green: medians scale with the denominator.
    assert verdicts([("op/linear", 1e6, 4e6, 1000, 4000)]) == [("GREEN", "op/linear")]
    # A quadratic reads red: medians scale with the denominator squared.
    assert verdicts([("op/quad", 1e6, 16e6, 1000, 4000)]) == [("RED", "op/quad")]
    # A sub-floor cell is skipped, never judged, even at a quadratic ratio.
    assert verdicts([("op/tiny", floor / 32, floor / 2, 1000, 4000)]) == [
        ("SKIP", "op/tiny")
    ]
    # The ceiling binds tightly: a hair under 1.3 is green, a hair over red.
    under = floor * 4 ** (MAX_WALL_SCALING_EXPONENT - 0.01)
    over = floor * 4 ** (MAX_WALL_SCALING_EXPONENT + 0.01)
    assert verdicts([("op/under", floor, under, 1000, 4000)]) == [("GREEN", "op/under")]
    assert verdicts([("op/over", floor, over, 1000, 4000)]) == [("RED", "op/over")]
    # The denominator, not the scale knob, is the fit's base: a family whose
    # bytes grow x16 across a x4 knob (the cliff genre) reads its honest
    # linear exponent against those bytes.
    assert verdicts([("op/cliff", 1e6, 16e6, 1000, 16000)]) == [("GREEN", "op/cliff")]
    # The committed tripwire's measured shape (benches/tripwire.rs via
    # `just bench-judge-tripwire`, quick mode, 2026-07-24: the machine-word
    # quadratic every board counter reads zero on — medians 239.39 us ->
    # 3.84 ms across n 512 -> 2048): red on the fitted exponent, ~2.0
    # against the 1.3 ceiling.
    rows = judge_cells(
        [("tripwire_unmetered_quadratic/quadratic", 239.39e3, 3.84e6, 512, 2048)]
    )
    [(verdict, _, exponent, _)] = rows
    assert verdict == "RED" and 1.8 <= exponent <= 2.2, rows
    # Malformed inputs are errors, never scores and never sub-floor skips:
    # a shrinking or flat denominator; a nonpositive median at either
    # scale (the hi side must NOT fall through to SKIP); a NaN or infinite
    # median at either scale.
    for bad in [
        ("op/shrunk", 1e6, 4e6, 4000, 1000),
        ("op/flat", 1e6, 4e6, 1000, 1000),
        ("op/lo_negative", -1.0, 4e6, 1000, 4000),
        ("op/lo_zero", 0.0, 4e6, 1000, 4000),
        ("op/hi_negative", 1e6, -5.0, 1000, 4000),
        ("op/hi_zero", 1e6, 0.0, 1000, 4000),
        ("op/lo_nan", float("nan"), 4e6, 1000, 4000),
        ("op/hi_nan", 1e6, float("nan"), 1000, 4000),
        ("op/hi_inf", 1e6, float("inf"), 1000, 4000),
        ("op/hi_not_a_number", 1e6, "4e6", 1000, 4000),
    ]:
        try:
            judge_cells([bad])
        except JudgeError:
            pass
        else:
            raise AssertionError(f"expected a JudgeError for {bad}")

    # The exit contract, end-to-end: build synthetic criterion trees and
    # assert what `main` RETURNS, so a judge that stops failing (the
    # `return 0 on red` mutation) cannot pass its own self-test.

    def stamp(scale, profile="optimized", sampling="quick", tip="selftest"):
        return {"scale": scale, "profile": profile, "sampling": sampling, "tip": tip}

    def exit_code(root, lo_sidecar, hi_sidecar, *extra):
        argv = [
            "benchjudge",
            "--criterion-dir",
            str(root),
            "--lo",
            "lo",
            "--hi",
            "hi",
            "--denoms-lo",
            str(lo_sidecar),
            "--denoms-hi",
            str(hi_sidecar),
            *extra,
        ]
        with contextlib.redirect_stdout(io.StringIO()):
            with contextlib.redirect_stderr(io.StringIO()):
                return main(argv)

    def write_pair(root, cells, stamp_lo=None, stamp_hi=None):
        """cells: name -> (median_lo, median_hi); x4 denominator span."""
        for name, (median_lo, median_hi) in cells.items():
            for baseline, median in (("lo", median_lo), ("hi", median_hi)):
                op, _, family = name.partition("/")
                cell_dir = Path(root, op, family, baseline)
                cell_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
                (cell_dir / "estimates.json").write_text(
                    json.dumps({"median": {"point_estimate": median}}),
                    encoding="utf-8",
                )
        paths = {}
        for baseline, denom, st in (
            ("lo", 1000, stamp_lo or stamp(1.0)),
            ("hi", 4000, stamp_hi or stamp(4.0)),
        ):
            path = Path(root, f"denoms-{baseline}.json")
            path.write_text(
                json.dumps(
                    {
                        "stamp": st,
                        "denominator_bytes": {name: denom for name in cells},
                    }
                ),
                encoding="utf-8",
            )
            paths[baseline] = path
        return paths

    def judged(cells, *extra, stamp_lo=None, stamp_hi=None, roster=None):
        with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as root:
            paths = write_pair(root, cells, stamp_lo=stamp_lo, stamp_hi=stamp_hi)
            if roster is not None:
                roster_path = Path(root, "roster.json")
                roster_path.write_text(json.dumps(roster), encoding="utf-8")
                extra = (*extra, "--roster", str(roster_path))
            return exit_code(root, paths["lo"], paths["hi"], *extra)

    linear = (floor * 10.0, floor * 40.0)
    quadratic = (floor * 10.0, floor * 160.0)
    at_ceiling = (floor * 10.0, floor * 10.0 * 4**MAX_WALL_SCALING_EXPONENT)
    sub_floor = (floor / 32, floor / 2)

    # Exit 0 on all-green.
    assert judged({"op/a": linear, "op/b": linear}) == 0
    # Exit 1 on a red in normal mode: the `return 0 on red` mutation dies here.
    assert judged({"op/a": linear, "op/quad": quadratic}) == 1
    # Exit 1 when a tripwire run judges no red: the tripwire has gone dark.
    assert judged({"op/a": linear}, "--expect-red") == 1
    # Exit 0 when the tripwire run reads its red.
    assert judged({"op/quad": quadratic}, "--expect-red") == 0
    # Exit 2 on input errors, end-to-end: a zero hi median, a negative hi
    # median, a NaN median, a missing median key, a missing baseline.
    assert judged({"op/a": (floor * 10.0, 0.0)}) == 2
    assert judged({"op/a": (floor * 10.0, -5.0)}) == 2
    assert judged({"op/a": (float("nan"), floor * 40.0)}) == 2
    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as root:
        paths = write_pair(root, {"op/a": linear})
        estimates = Path(root, "op", "a", "hi", "estimates.json")
        estimates.write_text(json.dumps({"mean": {"point_estimate": 1.0}}))
        assert exit_code(root, paths["lo"], paths["hi"]) == 2
        estimates.unlink()
        assert exit_code(root, paths["lo"], paths["hi"]) == 2
    # Exit 2 on stamp mismatches: disagreeing tips, a non-growing scale
    # pair, a profile mismatch, and an invocation tip the sidecars predate.
    assert judged({"op/a": linear}, stamp_hi=stamp(4.0, tip="other")) == 2
    assert judged({"op/a": linear}, stamp_hi=stamp(1.0)) == 2
    assert judged({"op/a": linear}, stamp_hi=stamp(4.0, profile="dev")) == 2
    assert judged({"op/a": linear}, "--tip", "newer") == 2
    assert judged({"op/a": linear}, "--tip", "selftest") == 0

    def roster(red=(), boundary=(), sampling="quick"):
        return {
            "configuration": {
                "profile": "optimized",
                "sampling": sampling,
                "scale_lo": 1.0,
                "scale_hi": 4.0,
            },
            "red": list(red),
            "boundary": list(boundary),
        }

    # Roster mode: exit 0 iff the verdict map matches the roster exactly.
    assert judged({"op/quad": quadratic, "op/a": linear}, roster=roster(["op/quad"])) == 0
    # A rostered red reading GREEN is a verdict-flip/liveness signal: exit 1.
    assert judged({"op/quad": linear}, roster=roster(["op/quad"])) == 1
    # A rostered red drifting below the judgment floor is a violation.
    assert judged({"op/quad": sub_floor}, roster=roster(["op/quad"])) == 1
    # An unrostered red is a violation.
    assert judged({"op/quad": quadratic}, roster=roster()) == 1
    # A boundary cell may read either verdict within the fit-noise band...
    assert judged({"op/edge": at_ceiling}, roster=roster(boundary=["op/edge"])) == 0
    over_band = (floor * 10.0, floor * 10.0 * 4**2.0)
    under_band = (floor * 10.0, floor * 40.0)
    # ...but not a verdict outside it (the cell genuinely moved)...
    assert judged({"op/edge": over_band}, roster=roster(boundary=["op/edge"])) == 1
    assert judged({"op/edge": under_band}, roster=roster(boundary=["op/edge"])) == 1
    # ...and never a sub-floor skip.
    assert judged({"op/edge": sub_floor}, roster=roster(boundary=["op/edge"])) == 1
    # Unrostered cells drift between GREEN and SKIP freely: both non-red.
    assert judged({"op/a": linear, "op/tiny": sub_floor}, roster=roster()) == 0
    # A roster pinned to a different configuration is refused: exit 2.
    assert judged({"op/a": linear}, roster=roster(sampling="record")) == 2
    # A roster naming a cell the run does not contain is an input error.
    assert judged({"op/a": linear}, roster=roster(["op/gone"])) == 2


def main(argv):
    if argv[1:] == ["--self-test"]:
        self_test()
        return 0
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="benchjudge", description=__doc__.splitlines()[0]
    )
    parser.add_argument("--criterion-dir", required=True)
    parser.add_argument("--lo", required=True, help="smaller-scale baseline name")
    parser.add_argument("--hi", required=True, help="larger-scale baseline name")
    parser.add_argument("--denoms-lo", required=True)
    parser.add_argument("--denoms-hi", required=True)
    parser.add_argument(
        "--tip",
        help="the source tip of this invocation; both sidecars must be stamped with it",
    )
    mode = parser.add_mutually_exclusive_group()
    mode.add_argument(
        "--roster",
        help="roster mode: enforce this committed expected-verdict roster",
    )
    mode.add_argument(
        "--expect-red",
        action="store_true",
        help="tripwire mode: succeed iff at least one judged cell is red",
    )
    args = parser.parse_args(argv[1:])
    try:
        stamps, cells = load_cells(
            args.criterion_dir,
            args.lo,
            args.hi,
            args.denoms_lo,
            args.denoms_hi,
            args.tip,
        )
        roster = None
        if args.roster is not None:
            roster = load_roster(args.roster)
            check_roster_configuration(
                args.roster, roster["configuration"], stamps[0], stamps[1]
            )
        rows = judge_cells(cells)
        violations = None if roster is None else roster_violations(roster, rows)
    except JudgeError as error:
        print(f"benchjudge: {error}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    counts = render(rows, sys.stdout)
    if args.expect_red:
        if counts["RED"] == 0:
            print(
                "benchjudge: --expect-red: no judged cell read red; "
                "the tripwire has gone dark",
                file=sys.stderr,
            )
            return 1
        return 0
    if violations is not None:
        if violations:
            print(file=sys.stdout)
            for violation in violations:
                print(f"benchjudge: roster violation: {violation}", file=sys.stdout)
            return 1
        print(
            f"benchjudge: roster {args.roster} satisfied "
            f"({len(roster['red'])} expected red, "
            f"{len(roster['boundary'])} boundary)",
            file=sys.stdout,
        )
        return 0
    return 1 if counts["RED"] > 0 else 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv))
